            <ArticleTitle>Headache from intracranial air after a lumbar epidural injection: subarachnoid or subdural?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>303-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">There is a growing awareness of the possibility of headache resulting from intracranial air secondary to identification of the epidural space by loss of resistance using air (LOR-A). Most reported cases have been attributed to subarachnoid air passage. A case is described of sudden headache following LOR-A.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Various features of the case suggest that the passage of air to the head was subdural rather than subarachnoid.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Recovery was uneventful.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
